    The jury is still out on whether or not "teleological evolution"
  or "evolutionary teleology" is a mistake, much less a philosophical
  mistake, much less a big philosophical mistake, much less the
  biggest philosophical mistake, much less the biggest philosophical
  mistake one can make (given analysis by _The_Anthropic_Principle_).
  Game theory has much more ancient roots, however Richard Nowakowski
  says in the "preface" of _Games_of_No_Chance_ (c1998) that:

       "Combinatorial Game Theory, as an academic discipline, is
        still in its infancy.  Many analyses of individual games
        have appeared in print, starting in 1902 with C.L.Bouton's
        analysis of the game of Nim.  (For exact references to the
        works mentioned here, please see A.Fraenkel's bibliography
        on pages 493-537 of this volume.)  It was not until the
        1930's that a consistent theory for impartial games was
        developed, independently, by R.Sprague and P.M.Grundy,
        later to be expanded and expounded upon by R.K.Guy and
        C.A.B.Smith. ..."

  VonNeumann and Morgenstern did not publish until 1953, though
  VonNeumann had published independently as early as 1928 with
  _Zur_Theorie_der_Gesellshaftsspiele_ (Math.Ann. 100, 295-320).
  Nietzsche died in 1900, however it would still be an untenable
  proposition to suppose that valid philosophy -must- incorporate
  game theory.  As Wittgenstein had noted, there is no game that
  is like all other games, so game theory cannot be an encompassing
  taxonomical scheme, a sentiment echoed by Godel's observations.


